---
// One statewide page, not one per county. The FAQ carries FAQPage schema, and
// the same schema on five URLs makes them compete with each other for the same
// query instead of one of them winning it. Nothing in the answers is
// county-specific — the statutes and the carrier rules are the same in Pinellas
// and Orange — so there is nothing four pages could have said that this one
// cannot.
//
// The page still has to lead to an inspector, and a statewide page cannot know
// which county the reader is in, so it ends with the county list rather than a
// single button.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { counties } from '../lib/counties';

const live = counties.filter((c) => c.status === 'live');
---

<Layout
  title="4-Point and Wind Mitigation Inspections in Florida | Verified Home Inspector"
  description="What Florida insurers want at renewal: a 4-point on an older home and a wind mitigation for storm discounts, both from a DBPR-licensed home inspector."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="county-tag">Florida</div>
      <h1>4-point and wind mitigation inspections</h1>
      <p style="color:var(--navy-soft);font-weight:500;margin-top:10px;max-width:68ch;">
        Most people book a home inspection once, before they buy a house. In Florida the
        inspections an insurer asks for come round again and again, for homeowners who have no
        intention of moving.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="legal" style="max-width:68ch;">
        <p>
          If your premium has gone up and your agent shops other carriers for you, the carrier
          that quotes a better rate will almost always want a current 4-point inspection before
          it writes the policy, and a wind mitigation inspection is what earns the
          storm-resistance discounts on top of that. Both are performed by a home inspector
          licensed by the Florida Department of Business and Professional Regulation, and both
          are usually needed again the next time you change carrier. That is why a homeowner who
          shops for a better rate every few years ends up hiring an inspector every few years
          without ever buying or selling a house.
        </p>
        <p>
          Roof age is the other reason a Florida homeowner suddenly needs an inspector.
          Section 627.7011 of the Florida Statutes says an insurer may not refuse to write or
          renew a policy solely because of the age of a roof that is less than 15 years old, and
          once a roof reaches 15 years the insurer must let you pay for an inspection before it
          can demand a replacement as a condition of coverage. If that inspection finds five or
          more years of useful life remaining, the insurer may not refuse solely because of the
          roof age. Subsection (5)(a) names a home inspector licensed under section 468.8314 as
          one of the professionals who can perform it — the same license every inspector in this
          directory holds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq />

  <section class="section" id="find-an-inspector">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Find a licensed inspector</h2>
        <div class="note">Every listing verified against the Florida DBPR record</div>
      </div>
      <p style="color:var(--slate);max-width:68ch;margin-top:0;">
        Requesting an inspector sends your details to that inspector and to nobody else. We never
        pass the same homeowner to several inspectors, and we never sell contact information.
      </p>
      <ul class="county-links">
        {live.map((c) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/fl/${c.slug}/#all-inspectors`}>
              {c.name}{c.metro ? ` · ${c.metro}` : ''}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
